Supreme People's Court, Supreme People's Procuratorate, Ministry of Public Security

Notice on Issuing the Provisions on Several Issues Concerning the Collection, Extraction, and Review and Determination of Electronic Data in Criminal Cases

Fa Fa [2016] No. 22

To the High People's Courts, People's Procuratorates, and Public Security Departments (Bureaus) of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army, the Military Procuratorate of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region, the People's Procuratorate of the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps, and the Public Security Bureau of the Xinjiang Production and Construction Corps:

To standardize the collection, extraction, review, and determination of electronic data and improve the quality of handling criminal cases, the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate, and the Ministry of Public Security have formulated the Provisions on Several Issues Concerning the Collection, Extraction, and Review and Determination of Electronic Data in Criminal Cases. We hereby issue them to you for earnest implementation. Issues encountered during implementation shall be promptly reported to the Supreme People's Court, the Supreme People's Procuratorate, and the Ministry of Public Security respectively.

September 9, 2016

Provisions on Several Issues Concerning the Collection, Extraction, and Review and Determination of Electronic Data in Criminal Cases

To standardize the collection, extraction, review, and determination of electronic data and improve the quality of handling criminal cases, these Provisions are formulated in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China and other relevant legal provisions, and in light of judicial practice.

1. General Provisions

Article 1 Electronic data refers to data formed during the occurrence of a case, stored, processed, or transmitted in digital form, that can prove the facts of the case.

Electronic data includes, but is not limited to, the following information and electronic documents:

(1) Information published on network platforms such as web pages, blogs, microblogs (Weibo), WeChat Moments (Pengyouquan), Tieba, and cloud storage;

(2) Communication information from network application services such as SMS (text messages), emails, instant messaging, and communication groups;

(3) Information such as user registration information, identity authentication information, electronic transaction records, communication records, and login logs;

(4) Electronic documents such as documents, images, audio/video, digital certificates, and computer programs.

Evidence such as witness testimony, victim statements, and confessions and explanations of criminal suspects and defendants, if recorded in digital form, does not constitute electronic data. Where truly necessary, the collection, extraction, transfer, and review of relevant evidence may be carried out with reference to these Provisions.

Article 2 Investigative organs shall follow statutory procedures and relevant technical standards to collect and extract electronic data comprehensively, objectively, and promptly; People's Procuratorates and People's Courts shall review and determine electronic data with a focus on authenticity, legality, and relevance.

Article 3 People's Courts, People's Procuratorates, and public security organs have the authority to collect and obtain electronic data from relevant entities and individuals in accordance with the law. The relevant entities and individuals shall truthfully provide such data.

Article 4 Where electronic data involves State secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy, it shall be kept confidential.

Article 5 For electronic data used as evidence, one or more of the following methods shall be adopted to protect the integrity of the electronic data:

(1) Seizing and sealing the original storage medium of the electronic data;

(2) Computing the integrity check value of the electronic data;

(3) Making and sealing a backup of the electronic data;

(4) Freezing the electronic data;

(5) Recording activities related to the collection and extraction of the electronic data by video;

(6) Other methods to protect the integrity of electronic data.

Article 6 Electronic data collected and extracted during the preliminary investigation, as well as electronic data extracted online via the network, may be used as evidence.

2. Collection and Extraction of Electronic Data

Article 7 Collection and extraction of electronic data shall be carried out by two or more investigators. The evidence collection methods shall comply with relevant technical standards.

Article 8 When collecting and extracting electronic data, if the original storage medium of the electronic data can be seized, the original storage medium shall be seized and sealed, and a record shall be made documenting the sealed state of the original storage medium.

When sealing the original storage medium of electronic data, it shall be ensured that, without releasing the seal, it is impossible to add, delete, or modify the electronic data. Before and after sealing, photographs of the sealed original storage medium shall be taken, clearly showing the condition of the seal opening or the affixed seal strip.

When sealing a storage medium with wireless communication functions, such as a mobile phone, measures such as signal shielding, signal blocking, or power disconnection shall be taken.

Article 9 Where the original storage medium cannot be seized under any of the following circumstances, the electronic data may be extracted, provided that the reasons for not being able to seize the original storage medium, the location of the original storage medium, or the source of the electronic data are noted in the record, and the integrity check value of the electronic data is computed:

(1) The original storage medium is inconvenient to seal;

(2) Extracting electronic data that is not stored on a storage medium, such as computer memory data or network transmission data;

(3) The original storage medium is located abroad;

(4) Other circumstances where the original storage medium cannot be seized.

For electronic data where the original storage medium is located abroad or on a remote computer information system, it may be extracted online via the network.

To further ascertain relevant circumstances, when necessary, a remote online inspection of the remote computer information system may be conducted. If technical investigation measures are required for the remote online inspection, strict approval procedures shall be followed in accordance with the law.

Article 10 Where it is objectively impossible or inappropriate to collect and extract electronic data in accordance with Articles 8 and 9, relevant evidence may be fixed by means such as printing, taking photographs, or recording video, and the reasons shall be stated in the record.

Article 11 Under any of the following circumstances, with the approval of the head of a public security organ at or above the county level or the Chief Procurator, the electronic data may be frozen:

(1) The data volume is large and cannot or is inconvenient to extract;

(2) The extraction time is long and may cause the electronic data to be tampered with or lost;

(3) The electronic data can be displayed more intuitively through network applications;

(4) Other circumstances requiring freezing.

Article 12 To freeze electronic data, a Notice of Assistance in Freezing shall be prepared, specifying information such as the network application account of the frozen electronic data, and served on the holder of the electronic data, the network service provider, or the relevant department for assistance in execution. To unfreeze, a Notice of Assistance in Unfreezing shall be prepared within three days and served on the holder of the electronic data, the network service provider, or the relevant department for assistance in execution.

To freeze electronic data, one or more of the following methods shall be adopted:

(1) Computing the integrity check value of the electronic data;

(2) Locking the network application account;

(3) Other measures to prevent the addition, deletion, or modification of electronic data.

Article 13 To obtain electronic data, a Notice of Obtaining Evidence shall be prepared, specifying the relevant information of the electronic data to be obtained, and notify the holder of the electronic data, the network service provider, or the relevant department to execute.

Article 14 When collecting and extracting electronic data, a record shall be made documenting the cause of the case, the object, the content, the time, place, method, and process of collecting and extracting the electronic data, accompanied by a list of electronic data specifying the category, file format, and integrity check value, and shall be signed or sealed by the investigator(s) and the holder (provider) of the electronic data; if the holder (provider) cannot sign or refuses to sign, it shall be noted in the record, and signed or sealed by the witness. Where conditions permit, the relevant activities shall be recorded by video.

Article 15 When collecting and extracting electronic data, a person who meets the conditions shall serve as a witness in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law. If it is objectively impossible to have a person who meets the conditions serve as a witness, the circumstances shall be noted in the record, and the relevant activities shall be recorded by video.

When collecting and extracting electronic data from multiple computer information systems at the same site, one witness may serve as the witness.

Article 16 The seized original storage medium or extracted electronic data may be examined through methods such as restoration, decryption, statistics, association, and comparison. When necessary, investigative experiments may be conducted.

For the examination of electronic data, the process of unsealing the electronic data storage medium shall be recorded by video, and the electronic data storage medium shall be connected to the examination device through a write-protection device for examination; where conditions permit, a backup of the electronic data shall be made and the backup shall be examined; if a write-protection device cannot be used and a backup cannot be made, the reasons shall be noted, and the relevant activities shall be recorded by video.

Examination of electronic data shall be documented in a record, specifying the examination method, process, and results, and shall be signed or sealed by the relevant personnel. If investigative experiments are conducted, a record of the investigative experiment shall be made, specifying the conditions, process, and results of the experiment, and shall be signed or sealed by the participants in the experiment.

Article 17 Where it is difficult to determine specialized issues involving electronic data, a judicial鉴定 institution shall issue an expert opinion, or an institution designated by the Ministry of Public Security shall issue a report. For cases directly accepted by the People's Procuratorate, an institution designated by the Supreme People's Procuratorate may also issue a report.

Specific measures shall be separately formulated by the Ministry of Public Security and the Supreme People's Procuratorate.

3. Transfer and Presentation of Electronic Data

Article 18 The collected or extracted original storage medium or electronic data shall be transferred with the case in a sealed state, and a backup of the electronic data shall be made and transferred together.

For electronic data that can be directly displayed, such as web pages, documents, and images, printouts may not be transferred with the case; if the People's Court or People's Procuratorate is unable to directly display the electronic data due to equipment or other limitations, the investigative organ shall transfer printouts with the case, or attach a display tool and instructions on the display method.

For frozen electronic data, a list of the frozen electronic data shall be transferred, specifying the category, file format, freezing entity, key points of evidence, relevant network application accounts, and accompanied by instructions on viewing tools and methods.

Article 19 For electronic data that cannot be directly displayed, such as programs, tools for intruding into or illegally controlling computer information systems, and computer viruses, a description of the attributes and functions of the electronic data shall be attached.

For issues such as data statistics and data identity, the investigative organ shall issue a statement.

Article 20 When a public security organ submits a request to the People's Procuratorate for approval of arrest of a criminal suspect, or transfers a case after investigation to the People's Procuratorate for review and prosecution, it shall transfer electronic data and other evidence together with the case to the People's Procuratorate. If, during the process of reviewing the approval of arrest and reviewing prosecution, the People's Procuratorate discovers that electronic data that should have been transferred has not been transferred or that the transferred electronic data does not meet the relevant requirements, it shall notify the public security organ to supplement the transfer or make corrections.

For cases initiated for public prosecution, if the People's Court discovers that electronic data that should have been transferred has not been transferred or that the transferred electronic data does not meet the relevant requirements, it shall notify the People's Procuratorate.

The public security organ or the People's Procuratorate shall transfer the electronic data or supplement the relevant materials within three days from the receipt of the notice.

Article 21 If electronic data submitted to the court by the prosecution and the defense needs to be presented, it may be displayed, played, or demonstrated with the aid of multimedia equipment, depending on the specific type of electronic data. When necessary, a person with specialized knowledge may be engaged to operate the equipment and provide explanations on relevant technical issues.

4. Review and Determination of Electronic Data

Article 22 When reviewing whether electronic data is authentic, the following content shall be focused on:

(1) Whether the original storage medium has been transferred; if the original storage medium cannot be sealed or is inconvenient to move, whether reasons are provided, and the collection and extraction process, the location of the original storage medium, or the source of the electronic data are specified;

(2) Whether the electronic data has special identifiers such as digital signatures or digital certificates;

(3) Whether the process of collecting and extracting the electronic data can be reproduced;

(4) If there are circumstances such as addition, deletion, or modification of the electronic data, whether an explanation is attached;

(5) Whether the integrity of the electronic data can be guaranteed.

Article 23 Whether the electronic data is complete shall be verified according to the corresponding methods for protecting the integrity of electronic data:

(1) Review the seizure and sealing status of the original storage medium;

(2) Review the collection and extraction process of the electronic data, and view the video recordings;

(3) Compare the integrity check value of the electronic data;

(4) Compare with the backup of the electronic data;

(5) Review the access operation logs after freezing;

(6) Other methods.

Article 24 When reviewing whether the collection and extraction of electronic data is lawful, the following content shall be focused on:

(1) Whether the collection and extraction of electronic data was carried out by two or more investigators, and whether the evidence collection method complies with relevant technical standards;

(2) Whether the collection and extraction of electronic data is accompanied by a record and a list, signed or sealed by the investigator(s), the holder (provider) of the electronic data, and the witness; if there is no signature or seal of the holder (provider), whether the reasons are noted; whether the category, file format, etc. of the electronic data are clearly recorded;

(3) Whether a person who meets the conditions served as a witness in accordance with the relevant provisions, and whether the relevant activities were recorded by video;

(4) Whether the examination of electronic data connected the electronic data storage medium to the examination device through a write-protection device; if conditions permit, whether a backup of the electronic data was made and the backup was examined; if a backup cannot be made and a write-protection device cannot be used, whether a video recording is attached.

Article 25 The identity between the network identity and the real identity of a criminal suspect or defendant may be determined through comprehensive assessment of relevant IP addresses, network activity records, attribution of the terminal used to access the internet, relevant witness testimony, and the confessions and explanations of the criminal suspect or defendant.

The connection between a criminal suspect or defendant and a storage medium may be determined through comprehensive assessment of relevant witness testimony and the confessions and explanations of the criminal suspect or defendant.

Article 26 If the public prosecutor, the parties, or the defender, or the agent ad litem object to the expert opinion on electronic data, they may apply to the People's Court to notify the expert to appear in court to testify. If the People's Court considers it necessary for the expert to appear, the expert shall appear in court to testify.

If, after being notified by the People's Court, the expert refuses to appear in court to testify without justifiable reasons, the expert opinion shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case. For an expert who refuses to appear in court to testify without justifiable reasons, the People's Court shall notify the judicial administrative organ or the relevant department.

The public prosecutor, the parties, or the defender, or the agent ad litem may apply to the court to notify a person with specialized knowledge to appear in court to provide opinions on the expert opinion.

For reports on specialized issues involving electronic data, the preceding three paragraphs apply mutatis mutandis.

Article 27 If there are defects in the collection and extraction procedures of electronic data, the electronic data may be adopted after corrections or reasonable explanations are provided; if corrections cannot be made or reasonable explanations cannot be provided, they shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:

(1) Failure to transfer in a sealed state;

(2) Absence of signature or seal of the investigator(s), the holder (provider) of the electronic data, or the witness on the record or list;

(3) Unclear description of the name, category, format, etc. of the electronic data;

(4) Other defects.

Article 28 Electronic data under any of the following circumstances shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:

(1) The electronic data has been tampered with, forged, or its authenticity cannot be determined;

(2) There are circumstances such as addition, deletion, or modification of the electronic data that affect its authenticity;

(3) Other circumstances where the authenticity of the electronic data cannot be guaranteed.

5. Supplementary Provisions

Article 29 The meanings of the following terms used in these Provisions are:

(1) "Storage medium" refers to carriers such as electronic devices, hard drives, optical discs, USB flash drives, memory sticks, memory cards, and memory chips that have the function of storing data information.

(2) "Integrity check value" refers to a data value used to verify the integrity of electronic data, computed by using a specific algorithm such as a hash algorithm on the electronic data, to prevent tampering or destruction.

(3) "Remote online inspection" refers to an investigative activity that conducts an inspection of a remote computer information system through the network, discovering and extracting electronic data related to the crime, recording the state of the computer information system, determining the nature of the case, analyzing the criminal process, identifying the direction and scope of the investigation, and providing clues and evidence for solving the case and criminal proceedings.

(4) "Digital signature" refers to a data value used to verify the source and integrity of electronic data, computed by using a specific algorithm on the electronic data.

(5) "Digital certificate" refers to an electronic file that contains a digital signature and certifies the source and integrity of electronic data.

(6) "Access operation log" refers to a detailed record automatically generated by a computer information system of access and operations on electronic data, for the purpose of reviewing whether the electronic data has been added, deleted, or modified.

Article 30 These Provisions shall come into effect on October 1, 2016. Where any previously issued normative documents are inconsistent with these Provisions, these Provisions shall prevail.
